    deepExplanation: "Step 1 — Understand the FIFO rule:\nThe first item inserted is the first item removed.\n\nWITHOUT BUILT-IN / CORE:\n```ts\nclass Queue<T> {\n  private readonly items: T[] = [];\n  private frontIndex = 0;\n\n  enqueue(value: T): void {\n    this.items.push(value);\n  }\n\n  dequeue(): T | undefined {\n    if (this.frontIndex >= this.items.length) {\n      return undefined;\n    }\n\n    const value = this.items[this.frontIndex];\n    this.frontIndex++;\n\n    if (this.frontIndex === this.items.length) {\n      this.items.length = 0;\n      this.frontIndex = 0;\n    }\n\n    return value;\n  }\n\n  peek(): T | undefined {\n    return this.items[this.frontIndex];\n  }\n\n  isEmpty(): boolean {\n    return this.frontIndex >= this.items.length;\n  }\n}\n```\n\nWITH BUILT-IN:\n```ts\nfunction processQueueUsingBuiltIns(values: readonly number[]): number[] {\n  const queue = [...values];\n  const result: number[] = [];\n\n  while (queue.length > 0) {\n    result.push(queue.shift()!);\n  }\n\n  return result;\n}\n```\n\nStep 2 — Example:\nenqueue(10), enqueue(20), enqueue(30)\nqueue = [10,20,30]\ndequeue() → 10\ndequeue() → 20\n\nStep 3 — Complexity:\nCore queue enqueue O(1) amortized and dequeue O(1) with a front index.\nSpace O(n).\n\nStep 4 — Interview rule:\nFor large queues, prefer a front pointer rather than repeated shift().",
