    expectedAnswer: "A deque (double-ended queue) supports insertion and removal at both the front and back. It can model a stack, queue, or sliding-window structure and is the natural abstraction behind many monotonic deque problems.",
    deepExplanation: "Step 1 — Required operations:\npushFront, pushBack, popFront, popBack, peekFront, peekBack.\n\nWITHOUT BUILT-IN / CORE:\n```ts\nclass Deque<T> {\n  private readonly items: Array<T | undefined> = [];\n  private front = 0;\n  private back = 0;\n\n  pushBack(value: T): void {\n    this.items[this.back] = value;\n    this.back++;\n  }\n\n  pushFront(value: T): void {\n    this.front--;\n    this.items[this.front] = value;\n  }\n\n  popFront(): T | undefined {\n    if (this.front >= this.back) return undefined;\n\n    const value = this.items[this.front];\n    this.items[this.front] = undefined;\n    this.front++;\n\n    return value;\n  }\n\n  popBack(): T | undefined {\n    if (this.front >= this.back) return undefined;\n\n    this.back--;\n    const value = this.items[this.back];\n    this.items[this.back] = undefined;\n\n    return value;\n  }\n\n  peekFront(): T | undefined {\n    return this.items[this.front];\n  }\n\n  peekBack(): T | undefined {\n    return this.front < this.back\n      ? this.items[this.back - 1]\n      : undefined;\n  }\n}\n```\n\nWITH BUILT-IN-friendly:\n```ts\nfunction dequeFromValues<T>(values: readonly T[]): T[] {\n  return [...values];\n}\n```\n\nStep 2 — Example:\n[2,3]\npushFront(1) → [1,2,3]\npushBack(4) → [1,2,3,4]\npopFront() → 1\npopBack() → 4\nremaining [2,3]\n\nStep 3 — Complexity:\nEach end operation is O(1) in the custom implementation.\nSpace O(n).\n\nStep 4 — Interview rule:\nUse a deque when both ends matter; use a normal queue when only FIFO behavior is required.",
