    deepExplanation: "Step 1 — Start with a FIFO task queue.\n\nWITHOUT BUILT-IN / CORE:\n```ts\ntype Task<T> = () => Promise<T>;\n\nclass TaskQueue {\n  private readonly queue: Array<() => Promise<void>> = [];\n  private front = 0;\n  private running = 0;\n\n  constructor(private readonly concurrency: number) {\n    if (concurrency <= 0) {\n      throw new Error(\"concurrency must be positive\");\n    }\n  }\n\n  add(task: () => Promise<void>): void {\n    this.queue.push(task);\n    this.drain();\n  }\n\n  private drain(): void {\n    while (\n      this.running < this.concurrency &&\n      this.front < this.queue.length\n    ) {\n      const task = this.queue[this.front++]!;\n      this.running++;\n\n      void task()\n        .catch(() => {\n          // Production code should record/log the task failure.\n        })\n        .finally(() => {\n          this.running--;\n\n          if (this.front === this.queue.length) {\n            this.queue.length = 0;\n            this.front = 0;\n          }\n\n          this.drain();\n        });\n    }\n  }\n}\n```\n\nWITH BUILT-IN-friendly:\n```ts\nasync function runTasksSequentially(\n  tasks: readonly (() => Promise<void>)[],\n): Promise<void> {\n  for (const task of tasks) {\n    await task();\n  }\n}\n```\n\nStep 2 — Example:\nconcurrency=2\nA,B start\nC waits\nWhen A finishes → C starts.\n\nStep 3 — Complexity:\nQueue operations are O(1) amortized.\nMemory depends on pending task count.\n\nStep 4 — Production rule:\nFor real systems also design retry, cancellation, timeout, backpressure, and observability.",
