    deepExplanation: "Step 1 — Model the shared queue.\n\nWITHOUT BUILT-IN / CORE:\n```ts\nclass ProducerConsumerQueue<T> {\n  private readonly items: T[] = [];\n  private front = 0;\n\n  enqueue(value: T): void {\n    this.items.push(value);\n  }\n\n  dequeue(): T | undefined {\n    if (this.front >= this.items.length) return undefined;\n\n    const value = this.items[this.front++];\n\n    if (this.front === this.items.length) {\n      this.items.length = 0;\n      this.front = 0;\n    }\n\n    return value;\n  }\n\n  size(): number {\n    return this.items.length - this.front;\n  }\n}\n```\n\nWITH BUILT-IN-friendly async consumer:\n```ts\nasync function consumeAll<T>(\n  queue: T[],\n  consume: (value: T) => Promise<void>,\n): Promise<void> {\n  for (const value of queue) {\n    await consume(value);\n  }\n}\n```\n\nStep 2 — Example:\nProducer adds A,B,C.\nConsumer removes A first, then B, then C.\n\nStep 3 — Backpressure:\nIf queue size reaches a configured maximum, the producer should pause, reject, or slow down.\n\nStep 4 — Production considerations:\nUse explicit lifecycle, failure handling, retries, cancellation, and observability.",
